{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for criminal conviction, civil liability, or human trafficking","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of or civilly liable for assault, threats, theft, drugs, or human trafficking, or who permits such a person to reside on the premises; a three-day Notice to Quit and a two-year filing limit apply.","key_value":"3-day notice; 2-year limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in residential dwellings where the landlord alleges a covered conviction, civil liability, or human trafficking violation, including permitting a person with such a conviction to reside on the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after : the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release fr om incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three days prior to filing suit for eviction (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(p)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling eviction causes and notice","requirement":"A landlord of an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may evict only for causes: holdover, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, rule violations, or lease violations. Notice is three months for at-will or year-to-year, one month for month-to-month, or three days for disorderly, destructive, or rule/lease violations.","key_value":"3-month notice (at-will/year-to-year); 1-month (month-to-month); 3-day (disorderly etc.)","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings. A court order is required; specific causes and notice periods apply.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction good cause and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise. They must also give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, subject to statutory exceptions.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming or boarding house"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85% temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3; N.J.A.C. 5:26-3.4","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations: owner occupancy and permanent removal","requirement":"A landlord personally occupying a unit after a tenant vacates must occupy it six months or pay three times damages plus attorney fees. Permanently removing a building from residential use bars residential use for five years and may bring a $10,000 civil penalty per violation.","key_value":"6 months; 5 years; 3x damages; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who evict tenants based on notice of personal occupancy or permanent removal from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation for residential use during five-year period.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal eviction prohibition","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, lease alteration, or refusal to renew when a tenant exercises civil rights, including enforcing lease or law rights, complaining to a government authority after written notice to the landlord, or joining a tenant organization.","key_value":"no reprisal; damages","coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction protections","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; if a foreclosed-property buyer wants to occupy the unit, the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a lease beyond 90 days must expire first. New owners must send notices within 10 business days.","key_value":"90-day notice; 10 business days","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt state or local laws with longer time periods or additional tenant protections.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Qualified seniors, disabled tenants, or certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is at or below three times county average per person income or $50,000. Applications are generally due within 60 days.","key_value":"40 years; income ≤ 3x county average or $50,000; 60-day application","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before conversion recording; permanently disabled; or honorably discharged disabled veterans 60% or more; lived in building at least one year before conversion recording.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"conversion recording date; tenant lived in building at least one year prior"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"Extends protected tenancy protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act. Currently the only qualified county is Hudson County.","key_value":"Hudson County only","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase prohibition under rent control or unconscionable increase","requirement":"A landlord may not increase rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, if the property is not subject to rent control, impose an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental properties subject to a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; otherwise, properties not subject to rent control as to unreasonable or unconscionable increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
